15.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the English tradition the inspector is Ochumelov / Otchumyelov and the garment that is repeatedly invoked is his  шинель, his police great-coat / summer overcoat.

The garment is not a piece of stage business but a running barometer of the inspector’s self-importance and of the fear that makes him change colour. It is invoked on his first appearance on the scene and then every time his attitude to the dog, to Khryukin and to the possible intervention of Gen. Zhigalov changes.

In the usual Constance Garnett / Wilks text that is four explicit mentions of the coat, three of them a distinct symbolic re-description:

* the entry in the coat, the confident, “official” Ochumelov;
* the fumbling / tugging at the coat when the crowd’s hostility and the possibility that the dog belongs to a general make him nervous;
* the re-buttoning / re-adjusting of the coat when he has decided to be severe with Khryukin again;
* the final walk-off in the coat, the chameleon safely back in his official shell.

It is those three meaningful shifts, normally quoted as the three/four mentions of the шинель, that are conventionally read as the coat standing in for Ochumelov’s fearful, self-serving mentality.

