12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the symbolic reading that the criticism always quotes the шинель / overcoat is not an inventory item that Chekhov keeps naming, it is an objective correlative that is re-written each time Ochumelov / Otchumyelov re-writes his own opinion.

In the canonical Russian text and in Garnett/Wilks the garment is named five times in the Russian, four times in the usual English translation, and those mentions collapse into three substantive descriptive beats that carry the chameleon symbolism:

* Entrance / authoritative pose. He is brought on in the new / astrakhan-collared / buttoned-up overcoat, the “official” Ochumelov who can afford magnanimity and talk about the law;
* Mid-scene agitation / deference. As the crowd presses him and the possibility of General Zhigalov’s dog is raised the coat is loosened / fiddled with / smoothed / worried about being soiled, the physical sign of nervous self-protection;
* Exit / servile retreat. On the final reversal the coat is re-buttoned / straightened / thrown open / held tight as he hurries away, the same garment now underscoring embarrassed, abject capitulation.

Those are the three moments where Chekhov actually describes the coat as a symbol of the fearful, opportunistic mentality and its successive shifts. The entrance presentation and the final walk-off are often counted as a fourth lexical mention of the overcoat/shinel in English editions, and five lexical occurrences in the Russian original, but descriptively they are the three core symbolic re-descriptions that the story uses as the visual chameleon for Ochumelov’s mood.

6.text:
In the 1884 Russian text and in the Garnett/Wilks English tradition the шинель/overcoat/greatcoat is not a piece of static inventory. It is an objective correlative for Ochumelov’s chameleon-like fear and it is re-written each time his self-assurance is rebuilt on a new social identification.

There are four lexical touches of the garment in the scene and five lexical occurrences of шинель in the Russian original, but only three of those are substantive descriptive re-presentations of the coat as a symbolic barometer of his mentality:

* the entrance presentation – the official in the new / shabby / buttoned-up greatcoat with collar up and hands in pockets – the confident, self-important baseline;
* the agitated middle – the coat loosened/unbuttoned, fiddled with, brushed, the collar worried about being soiled, the hand-in-pocket manoeuvring when the dog may belong to General Zhigalov and he is afraid of committing himself;
* the final re-composition – the coat re-buttoned / straightened / pulled tight again on the reversal to contempt for the stray and the hurried exit in a now rumpled/deferential garment.

Those are the three descriptive beats that criticism invariably isolates as the coat changing colour with Ochumelov’s opinion. The fourth textual touch is the closing re-mention / return handling of the same image on exit, i.e. a referential closure rather than a new descriptive portrait. Hence the canonical reading is three meaningful symbolic descriptions of the coat, realized in most English editions as four explicit mentions of the overcoat/greatcoat, five lexical mentions of шинель in Russian.

